> Still,  a definite seed of an idea here ... maybe one of Uncle
> Andrew's Rings (see: Magician's Nephew) which, when #invoked, takes
> one to 'the wood between the worlds' -- a one-level branch with about
> 12 magic portals . Each portal would take one to a dungeon branch or
> to a 'special' level -- some nice, some useless but pretty, some
> downright dangerous.  Which portal leads where would be randomized
> between games, so that it would have to be mapped out.  Making some of
> the branches lead to dangerous places (for instance, Demogorgon's
> lair) would discourage someone from using it before prepared to tackle
> the potential hazards.

This is a nice idea. The wood between the worlds itself would have to
be benign though- the artifact is supposed to be *useful* after all.
Having an instant-access level with lots of handy map features and no
nasty monsters would be great for resting up when HP are low, for a
stash location and for tinkering with inventory. All the (beeless)
trees in the wood would be handy as a food source, and you could have
a stream or pool in the wood for #dipping. An altar as well would be
ideal, but maybe overkill, especially given that at least one of the
portals is likely to put you near an altar or temple.

Maybe have it so that only the artifact's own class gets a monster-
free wood. If another class gets the ring via bones/ wish, then the
wood will spawn hostile monsters (preferably elves, nymphs, killer
bees...)

> Another aspect might be to add a couple new one-branch levels that are>
> than the total number of portal locations, then randomize which
> special levels were available in a game (although the dungeon branches
> would always be available) .  This would mean one would have to visit
> the Wood in multiple games to see everything.

Yes, absolutely. If you need inspiration for special one-level
branches, raid slashem: That has loads: A cave of dragons, Grund the
Orc King's fortress, a Giant-themed branch, Shelob's lair, a few
temple branches, 3 aligned quests and more.

Also, you could have a (very small) chance that a portal will lead to
some other class' quest nemesis level, giving the player a chance to
win someone else's quest artifact. That would make this ring quite a
desirable item, although the chance of it paying off would be small:
First, the chance of a quest level being generated would be limited,
and then even if you get one and beat the bad guy you might not be of
the right alignment to pick up and use the artifact anyway... This
could be a pain, actually, since it means another (potentially
useless) artifact is generated the second you step through the portal,
thus reducing your artifact-wishing chances. Another reason not to
explore the portals prematurely I suppose.


